GIVETH A Bar
DECIDE `ch 41 -- the body or goods of the detained merchant may be injured` IS
    IF   `the person is a merchant`
     AND `it is a time of war`
     AND `the merchant is of the land at war with the King`
    THEN IF `the merchant was found in the land at the beginning of the war`
         THEN IF `information has been received by the King or his chief justiciar of how the merchants of our land found in the land at war with us are treated`
              -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "until information be received by us, or by our chief justiciar".
              THEN `Not barred`
              ELSE Barred
         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "And if such are found in our land at the beginning of the war".
         ELSE `Not barred`
    -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: if, until because "such" are the merchants excepted in 41.2, and the branch is reached by a person who is not one of them).
    ELSE `Bar not covered`

-- 41.4: "if our men are safe there, the others shall be safe in our land".  The "if" is the text's
-- own condition, so if our men are not safe there the answer is `Not entitled` (fork F41b, F64d).
-- src:89 | and if our men are safe there, the others shall be safe in our land.
@ref Chapter 41 clause 41.4 (src:89)
GIVEN `the person is a merchant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is a time of war` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the merchant is of the land at war with the King` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the merchant was found in the land at the beginning of the war` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the merchants of our land are safe in the land at war with us` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN Entitlement
DECIDE `ch 41 -- the enemy merchant is safe in our land` IS
    IF   `the person is a merchant`
     AND `it is a time of war`
     AND `the merchant is of the land at war with the King`
     AND `the merchant was found in the land at the beginning of the war`
    THEN IF `the merchants of our land are safe in the land at war with us`
         THEN Entitled
         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "and if our men are safe there, the others shall be safe in our land".
         ELSE `Not entitled`
    -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: if because "if our men are safe" is the condition and has its own branch; this branch is reached by a person who is not one of the enemy merchants found at the beginning of the war, whom "the others" does not name).
    ELSE `Entitlement not covered`

§§ `Chapter 42 -- leaving and returning`

-- Merchants are sent back to chapter 41 ("who shall be treated as is above provided").
-- "except for a short period in time of war, on grounds of public policy": both limbs are needed
-- (fork F42a).
-- src:91 | It shall be lawful in future for any one (excepting always those imprisoned or outlawed in accordance with the law of the kingdom, and natives of any country at war with us, and merchants, who shall be treated as is above provided) to leave our kingdom and to return, safe and secure by land and water,
-- src:91 | except for a short period in time of war, on grounds of public policy—
@ref Chapter 42 clause 42.1-42.2 (src:91)
GIVEN `the person is imprisoned or outlawed in accordance with the law of the kingdom` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is a native of a country at war with the King` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is a merchant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is a short period in time of war` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the restriction is on grounds of public policy` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN Entitlement
DECIDE `ch 42 -- the person may leave the kingdom and return, safe and secure by land and water` IS
    IF   `the person is imprisoned or outlawed in accordance with the law of the kingdom`
      OR `the person is a native of a country at war with the King`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "excepting always those imprisoned or outlawed ... and natives of any country at war with us" and "except for a short period in time of war, on grounds of public policy".
    THEN `Not entitled`
    ELSE IF `the person is a merchant`
         -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: except, excepting, provided because these words send merchants back to chapter 41 ("as is above provided"); chapter 42 does not decide for them).
         THEN `Entitlement not covered`
         ELSE IF   `it is a short period in time of war`
               AND `the restriction is on grounds of public policy`
              -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "excepting always those imprisoned or outlawed ... and natives of any country at war with us" and "except for a short period in time of war, on grounds of public policy".
              THEN `Not entitled`
              ELSE Entitled

-- "reserving always the allegiance due to us": leaving does not release the allegiance.
-- src:91 | reserving always the allegiance due to us.
@ref Chapter 42 clause 42.3 (src:91)
GIVEN `the person owes allegiance to the King` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN Obligation
DECIDE `ch 42 -- the allegiance due to the King remains owed` IS
    IF `the person owes allegiance to the King`
    THEN Obliged
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text does not speak to a case outside the facts this rule names.
    ELSE `Obligation not covered`
